## 1. Introduction
Human survival and safety are seriously threated by the increasingly water pollution [1–3]. Thus, development of efficient water treatment technology to realize wastewater resource recovery is of great importance [4,5]. Membrane technology possesses many inimitable advantages, including easy operation, low energy consumption, high efficiency and environment-friendly, which has become one of the most competitive technologies for water treatment [6–8]. However, the inevitable membrane fouling largely raises the operating cost and is considered to be the bottleneck for industrial application of membrane technology [9,10]. The physicochemical interactions, especially the hydrophobicityhydrophobicity attractive forces between the hydrophobic membrane material and hydrophobic pollutants, was considered to be the main reason causing membrane fouling [11,12]. Given this, researchers have been devoted to improving the hydrophilicity of membranes to reduce water transport resistance and inhibit the adhesion of pollutants [6,13,14]. One of the most efficient methods is directly incorporating hydrophilic nanofillers into various membrane polymer matrices for improving their overall performances, especially water permeability and antifouling ability [15–17].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are comprised of organic linkers and metal ions, and considered as a typical porous crystalline material [18–20]. MOFs have been intensively explored as nanofillers for fabrication of polymeric membranes [21,22]. Compared with other traditional nanofillers, like zeolites [23], carbon nanotubes [24] and metallic oxides [25,26], MOFs possess better compatibility with the polymer matrix and high surface area as well as adjustable pore structure [27–30]. These advantages lead to the MOFs-based mixed matrix membranes with enhanced water permeability along with well-maintained rejection performance. However, most MOFs are hydrophobic, which may adversely affect membrane performance, especially antifouling ability [31,32]. From this point of view, appropriate surface modification is favorable to tailor the properties of MOFs for further optimizing the performance of MOFs-based mixed matrix membranes [33,34]. Moreover, although the better compatibility of MOFs enables it with good dispersibility in membrane matrix, most of MOFs particles usually distribute in membrane bulk due to their higher density [35]. The relatively low distribution on the membrane surface not only wastes material, but also hinders full display of potential effects of MOFs addition. Thus, developing strategies to balance the density effects and effectively manipulate distribution of MOFs in polymeric membrane matrix is highly desirable [36].
In current work, magnetic Ni@UiO-66 nanoparticles were synthesized through an in-situ reduction reaction and successfully incorporated into polyethersulfone (PES) membrane matrix during the phase conversion process. A magnetic field was applied to manipulate distribution of Ni@UiO-66 in the PES membrane due to its magnetic property. Briefly, the magnetic field was used to drag the magnetic Ni@UiO-66 nanoparticles onto membrane surface during the pahse inversion process. A series of characterizations were conducted to measure chemical composition and surface morphology of the prepared membranes. The filtration performance and antifouling ability were evaluated by filtrating typical model pollutants. Moreover, antifouling mechanism was explored by using the extended Derjaguin-Landau-VerweyOverbeek (XDLVO) theory. The structural control strategies proposed in this work may provide valuable guidance for development of high-performance MOFs-based mixed matrix membranes with efficient performance.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
West Asian reagent provided zirconium tetrachloride $\left(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\right)$ . Benzoic acid was purchased from Shanghai Merrill Reagent Co., Ltd. Terephthalic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{2}B D C)}$ was purchased from Aladdin Co., Ltd. Polyethersulfone (PES) was obtained from BASF Co., Ltd. Chemicals including sodium alginate (SA, 227.17 Da), humic acid (HA, $4000\sim185000~\mathrm{Da};$ , bovine serum albumin (BSA, 66430 Da), yeast extract (YEF), polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), silver nitrate $(\mathsf{A g N O}_{3})$ , nickel sulfate $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}S O_{4}{\cdot}6H_{2}O})^{\cdot}$ , dimethylaminoborane $(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{10}\mathsf{B N})$ , sodium pyrophosphate $(\mathsf{N a}_{4}\mathsf{P}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{7}{\cdot}10\mathsf{H}_{2}0);$ , ammonia $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0\right)$ , N. $N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) and N, N-dimethylacetamide (DMAC) were purchased from National Pharmaceutical Chemical Co., Ltd. Ultra-pure water used in this study was provided by a MilliPore-Q system.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Ni@UiO-66
UiO-66 was synthesized by a hydrothermal reaction. $\mathbf{0.396\g}$ $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ $1.7\ \mathrm{mmol};$ and $0.283\mathrm{~g~H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ $\cdot1.7\ \mathrm{mmol};$ were dissolved by $50~\mathrm{ml}$ DMF [37]. Thereafter, 50 equivalents of benzoic acid (relative to $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ ) were dissolved into the prepared solution. The mixture was then transferred to the $100\ \mathrm{ml}$ PTFE reaction still for reaction at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . UiO-66 can be obtained at end of the reaction. The prepared UiO-66 was washed by DMF and stored in an oven at $80^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ . Thereafter, $25\mathrm{gNi_{2}S O_{4}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ $50\mathrm{gNa}_{4}\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{\cdot}10\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{-}$ O, $1.5\mathrm{~g~C_{2}H_{10}B N}$ and 45 ml ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{0}$ were dissolved into 1L pure water to obtain the nickel bath. The prepared UiO-66 was added to the nickel bath to prepare ${\mathsf{N i@U i O–66}}$ by an in-situ reaction. Because the nickel components can endow UiO-66 with magnetism, the prepared Ni@UiO-66 nanoparticles can be easily separated by a magnet.
## 2.3. Preparation of composite membrane
The membranes were prepared by a phase inversion method. As shown in Fig. 1, the casting solution was firstly scraped on a glass plate and remained in the air for $30\:s$ , during which a flat magnet with an effective region of $15\mathrm{cm}\times10\mathrm{cm}$ (the maximum magnetic field intensity is 1500 gauss) was vertically set $1\ \mathrm{cm}$ above the glass plate. Under the magnetic force, the magnetic Ni@UiO-66 nanoparticles can move to the top layer of membrane. Furtherly, the magnetic Ni@UiO-66 nanoparticles can be finally anchored in the surface layer of membrane during the phase inversion process. Table 1 gives the composition of the casting solutions.
## 2.4. Characterizations
X-ray diffraction(XRD, Brook D8, Germany) patterns of MOFs crystals were acquired from $5^{\circ}$ to $60^{\circ}$ under the working conditions of $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $40~\mathrm{{mA}}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB 250 Xi, Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA) was utilized to examine the chemical composition of the materials and the prepared membranes. The test was adopted by ALK X-ray of $20~\mathrm{eV}$ and $1\times10^{-9}$ mbar. The morphology of the membrane was observed by scanning electron microscope (SEM, 73,447 Oberkochen). Element mapping tests was used to measure element distribution. The contact angles were tested by methylene diiodide, glycerol and deionized water droplets. Zeta potential data were obtained by Zeta 90 Plus and Anton Paar potential analyzer. These methods can be referred to previous literature [15,38–42].
## 2.5. Filtration tests
The filtration device used in this study is the dead-end filtration equipment. For all tests, the separation membrane was fixed in an ultrafilter cup and then filled with pure water and prepressed for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ at a pressure of $0.2\mathrm{MPa}$ to obtain a stable flux. After that, the membrane was used in the filtration experiment at $0.1~\mathrm{MPa}$ . After $15\mathrm{min}$ of filtration, the filtrate was collected to test the concentration after interception. Magnetic rotor stirring is used in all testing processes, and the stirring speed is $300~\mathrm{rpm}$ . The diameter of the magnetic rotor is $0.4~\mathrm{mm}$ and the length is $10\ \mathrm{mm}$ . Pure water flux $(J_{w},\mathrm{L}\mathsf{m}^{-2}\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ of membrane could be obtained from formula (1):
$$
J W=\frac{W}{A\times t}
$$
where $A,t$ and $W$ denote effective membrane area $(\mathsf{m}^{2})$ , test time (h) and permeated weight of pure water $(\mathrm{kg})$ , resepectively.
The pollutants used in this study are all single components. Rejection was tested by using the solutions of BSA $(1~\mathrm{g/L}$ , buffer reagent: $\mathrm{Na_{2}H P O_{4}\cdot12H_{2}O~21.847~g/L,~N a H_{2}P O_{4}\cdot2H_{2}O~6.084~g/L)}$
SA $(1\ \mathrm{g/L})$ , HA $(20\mathrm{mg/L},\mathrm{pH}=13\$ ) and YEF $(20\mathrm{mg/L})$ . Rejection rate R was obtained by formula (2):
$$
R=1-\frac{C_{1}}{C_{0}}\times100\%
$$
${\sf C}_{0}$ and $\mathsf{C}_{1}$ are the effluent concentration and original solution, respectively.
In this study, UV–vis spectrophotometer was used to analyze the rejection rate of BSA and HA at $280\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $254~\mathrm{nm}$ , and TOC analyzer was used to test the concentration of SA before and after filtration to obtain the rejection rate. Flux recovery rate (FRR) of membrane was calculated by formula (3):
$$
F R R=\frac{\mathrm{F_{1}}}{\mathrm{F_{0}}}\times100\%
$$
where $\mathrm{F}_{0}$ and $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ mean the original pure membrane flux and membrane flux at last filtration cycle before washing, respectively.
The steps for evaluating the photocatalytic self-cleaning performance of PES-Ni@UiO-66 modified membrane are similar to those of filtration experiments. After filtration for $6\mathrm{h}$ , the membrane was removed from the ultrafilter cup module. The membrane was then placed in a petri dish containing a small amount of pure water and irradiated under ultraviolet light source (UV, 36 W, $365\mathrm{nm}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to ahcieve photocatalytic self-cleaning. Then the membrane flux was measured by washing with ultra-pure water. Membrane self-cleaning ability was denoted by index of FRR.
## 2.6. Analysis by the XDLVO theory
The extended XDLVO theory can be used to quantitatively analyze thermodynamic interactions [43]. In this study, the thermodynamic interaction between membrane and four characteristic pollutants was quantitatively analyzed by evaluating acid-base interaction (AB), electrostatic interaction (EL), and van der Waals interaction (LW) [44,45]. When the separation distance between the two planes is $h$ , the interaction energy can be displayed as follows [46]:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\Delta G^{L W}(h)=\Delta G_{h_{0}}^{L W}\displaystyle\frac{h_{0}^{2}}{h^{2}}}}\\ {{}}\\ {{\Delta G^{A B}(h)=\Delta G_{h_{0}}^{A B}\displaystyle\mathrm{exp}(\displaystyle\frac{h_{0}-h}{\lambda})}}\\ {{}}\\ {{\Delta G^{E L}(h)=\kappa\zeta_{m}\zeta_{f}\displaystyle\varepsilon_{r}\varepsilon_{0}\left(\displaystyle\frac{\zeta_{m}^{2}+\zeta_{f}^{2}}{2\zeta_{m}\zeta_{f}}\right)\left(1-\coth\kappa h\right)+\displaystyle\frac{1}{\sinh\kappa h}}}\end{array}
$$
The minimum distance $\left(h_{0}\right)$ between two adjacent planes is $0.158~\mathrm{nm}$ . $\lambda$ $\left(=0.6\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ is the attenuation AB interaction length in water. $\varepsilon_{0}$ and $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{r}}$ are dielectric constant in vacuum and water, respectively. $\zeta_{m}$ and $\zeta_{f}$ denote zeta potential of membranes and sludge, respectively. $\kappa$ represents the reciprocal of Debye length and is derived from the following formula [47]:
$$
\kappa=\sqrt{e^{2}\sum n_{i}Z_{i}^{2}/\varepsilon_{r}\varepsilon_{0}\kappa T}
$$
Parameters values $\mathrm{of}\Delta G_{h_{0}}^{L W}$ , $\Delta G_{h_{0}}^{A B}$ 和 $\Delta G_{h_{0}}^{E L}$ are obtained according to the following formulas:
$$
\begin{array}{c}{{\Delta G_{h_{0}}^{A B}=2\left[\sqrt{\gamma_{w}^{+}}\left(\sqrt{\gamma_{f}^{-}}+\sqrt{\gamma_{m}^{-}}-\sqrt{\gamma_{w}^{-}}\right)+\sqrt{\gamma_{w}^{-}}\left(\sqrt{\gamma_{f}^{+}}+\sqrt{\gamma_{m}^{+}}-\sqrt{\gamma_{w}^{+}}\right)\right.}}\\ {{\left.\qquad-\sqrt{\gamma_{f}^{-}\gamma_{m}^{+}}-\sqrt{\gamma_{f}^{+}\gamma_{m}^{-}}\right]}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\Delta G_{h_{0}}^{E L}={\frac{\varepsilon_{0}\varepsilon_{r}\kappa}{2}}(\xi_{f}^{2}+\xi_{m}^{2})\left[1-\coth(\kappa h_{0})+{\frac{2\xi_{f}\xi_{m}}{\xi_{f}^{2}+\xi_{m}^{2}}}c s c h(\kappa h_{0})\right]
$$
$$
\Delta G_{h_{0}}^{L W}=-2\bigg(\sqrt{\gamma_{m}^{L W}}-\sqrt{\gamma_{w}^{L W}}\bigg)\bigg(\sqrt{\gamma_{f}^{L W}}-\sqrt{\gamma_{w}^{L W}}\bigg)
$$
where $\gamma^{+}$ and $\gamma^{-}$ denote electron acceptor and electron donor of a material, respectively. $\gamma\ ^{L W}$ represents LW component of surface tension. Subscript $f$ represents the pollutant. Subscript $w$ represents water and $m$ represents membrane. The contact angles of three specific liquids can be used to solve three Young’s equations shown as follows.
$$
\frac{(1+\mathrm{cos}\phi)}{2}\gamma_{l}^{T o l}=\sqrt{\gamma_{l}^{L W}}\sqrt{\gamma_{S}^{L W}}+\sqrt{\gamma_{l}^{-}}\sqrt{\gamma_{S}^{+}}+\sqrt{\gamma_{l}^{+}}\sqrt{\gamma_{S}^{-}}
$$
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of Ni@UiO-66
Fig. 2a and b reflect the TEM and SEM images of prepared UiO66. It is found that raw UiO-66 displays a typical three-dimensional octahedron, which agrees well with the crystal morphology of UiO66 reported before [37]. The SEM image and corresponding mapping images Ni@UiO-66 are presented in Fig. 2c–e. The uniform distribution of these elements illustrates that UiO-66 particles are uniformed coated by Ni after the in-situ reduction reaction. To further verify the coating of Ni on UiO-66, chemical compositions of UiO-66 and Ni@UiO-66 were analyzed by XPS, and the results are shown in Fig. 3a–e. The newly emerged $\mathsf{N i2p}$ peak in the Ni@UiO-66 XPS spectra are comparable with that of the original UiO-66 (Fig. 3a), indicating the successful surface coating of Ni on UiO-66. The nearly unchanged Zr 3d peak (Fig. 3b and d) and O 1 s peak (Fig. 3c and e) imply that the chemical composition of
UiO-66 was well maintained during the in-situ reduction reaction [48–50]. The modification of Ni also endows the resultant Ni@UiO66 with fascinating magnetism. Thus, it is possible to manipulate the movement of the Ni@UiO-66 with an external magnetic field (Fig. 3f).
## 3.2. Characterization of Ni@UiO-66 incorporated PES membranes
Fig. 4 shows the membrane surface morphology and crosssection images. The bare PES membrane (M0) exhibits a relatively smooth surface (Fig. 4a). In contrast, the Ni@UiO-66 incorporated PES membrane (M1) shows a rougher structure (Fig. 4c and 4e). which was consistent with the previous reports [46,51–53]. Surface roughness is even higher for the M2 membrane where the Ni@UiO-66 was dragged onto upper surface by an external magnetic field. This result indicates that the prepared magnetic Ni@UiO-66 particles are successfully concentrated on the surface of the membrane by magnetic force. The change of membrane porosity can be seen clearly through the SEM image (Fig. 5) of the membrane surface. In comparing, the porosity of M2 is obviously higher than that of M0. Fig. 6 shows the element mapping images of the M1 and M2 membranes’ cross-section. The uniform distribution of Zr and Ni elements in the cross-section of M1 indicates that Ni@UiO-66 is well dispersed in the membrane matrix, resulting from the superior compatibility of Ni@UiO-66. Nevertheless, different from M1, more Zr and Ni elements can be observed around the ‘‘skin” layer of M2 (Fig. 6c and d), which further proves the magnetic field pulling effect on magnetic Ni@UiO-66 particles. The reason of the inhomogenous distribution of nanoparticles could be the agglomerate of the nanoparticles, which is a general problem in nano field. Although the agglomerate limited the effects of nanoparticles, the total result was that the magnetic UiO-66 particles promoted the membrane performances.
XPS spectra can be used to analyze the chemical composition of membrane surfaces within several nanometers. As shown in Fig. 7, there is no signal of Zr and Ni of the original PES membrane, while three new peaks corresponding to $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ and $\mathsf{N i2p}$ show up in the XPS spectrum of M1 and M2. This result implies that Ni@UiO-66 is successfully incorporated into PES membrane. Moreover, the peak intensity of $\mathtt{\Omega}\mathtt{0}\mathtt{1}\mathtt{s},\mathtt{Z r}\mathtt{4p}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ of M2 is relatively higher than M1, which also indicated the successful distribution of Ni@UiO66 around the membrane surface driven by magnetic force. It’s worth noting that the peak of Ni 2p cannot be observed in the XPS spectra of M1 and M2, which may be attributed to its relatively low content.
Contact angles measurements were carried out to evaluate surface hydrophilicity of the prepared membranes (Fig. 8). The original PES membrane is relatively hydrophobic with a water contact angle (WCA) of $81.1^{\circ}$ . Due to the incorporating of hydrophilic ${\mathsf{N i@U i O–66}}$ and increased surface roughness, the Ni@UiO-66 incorporated PES membrane (M1) presents much higher hydrophilicity with a WCA of $61.2^{\circ}$ . As discussed above, compared with M1, more Ni@UiO-66 particles are exposed on the surface of M2 resulting from the magnetic force. Thus, the hydrophilicity of M2 was further enhanced with WCA decreased to $54.5^{\circ}$ , which is $26.6^{\circ}$ lower than that of M0. The enhanced hydrophilicity is certainly conducive to promoting water permeability and antifouling performance [54–56].
## 3.3. Separation performance of Ni@UiO-66 incorporated PES membranes
Fig. 9 depicts the performance data of the prepared membranes in terms of flux and rejection rate. The M2 membrane exhibits the maximum pure water flux value of $611.5\pm19.8~\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , quite higher than $271.5\pm14.7\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ for the M0 membrane. Interestingly, the rejection performance of M2 for BSA, SA and HA can be well maintained, indicating that the Ni@UiO-66 incorporated PES membrane (M2) exhibits a good balance between water permeability and rejection ability. The low rejection of HA could be related to the molecular size, molecular shape and inherent property of HA. Generally, the water permeability of membrane is determined by porosity and hydrophilicity. As we have discussed above, the addition of Ni@MOFs and the action of the external magnetic field can not only increase the surface roughness of the membrane and the porosity around the ‘‘skin” layer, but also endow the resultant membrane with enhanced hydrophilicity. Thus, the prepared membrane possesses a more efficient filtration area and reduced water transport resistance [13,14], resulting in greatly improved water permeability. The rejection process is usually controlled by electrostatic interaction and size sieving effect. The surface Zeta potential has just slightly increased from $-14.80\mathrm{mV}$ of M0 to $-17.17\mathrm{mV}$ of M2 (Table 3). Since the rejection of modified membrane was not decreased, the increase of pure water flux of the modified membrane was due to the increase of porosity rather than the increase of membrane pore size with the addition of Ni@UiO-66. The addition of nanoparticles usually cause a high viscosity of casting solution, which slows down the phase transformation rate [57]. Through the viscometer test of the prepared membrane casting solution, it was found that the viscosity of M0 and M2 was 3289 and $4637~\mathrm{{mPas}}$ respectively. The increase of viscosity just provided time for the hydrophilic nanoparticles to spread to the membrane surface [58].
## 3.4. Antifouling and self-cleaning performance
Antifouling performance of the pristine and two Ni@UiO-66 incorporated PES membranes were evaluated through cyclic filtration using four typical pollutants (BSA, SA, HA, and YEF). As shown in Fig. 10, the rapid decline of flux at the beginning of the filtration process can be attributed to the membrane pore blocking by these pollutant molecules, and the membranes were quickly fouled. After membrane rinsing process, the flux of the membranes can only recover to a certain extent due to the severe membrane fouling, especially for BSA, HA and YEF. Compared with the other two membranes, the more hydrophilic M2 always possesses the highest flux and FRR, indicating its better antifouling ability. Ni@UiO66 magnetic nanoparticles are embedded in the matrix of PES membranes. Compared with the surface coating, they have better stability and are not easy to fall off. We have carried out stability tests and found that the prepared materials revealed good stability in the long-term process. Moreover, a large number of literatures have verified that the stability of UiO-66 in long-term use and aging experiments was reliable [59,60].
The Zr elements in MOF have been proved to be a good photocatalyst which made the Ni@UiO-66 with UV responsiveness [61]. In addition, the group VIII element Ni also has photocatalysis function. Therefore, the Ni@UiO-66 particles in our current work revealed good photocatalyst. In view of the photocatalytic ability of Ni@UiO-66, the prepared membranes should possess photocatalytic self-cleaning ability, which was examined through a fourstage filtration process in this study. As shown in Fig. 11, due to the deposition of these pollutants on the membrane surface or trapping in the membrane pores, the surface rinsing process can not entirely recover the membrane’s original water permeability. However, after a certain time of UV irradiation, a much higher permeation flux increment can be observed for PES-Ni@UiO-66 membrane but not for the original PES membrane, indicating its photocatalytic self-cleaning ability. Compared with M1, even M2 has the same Ni@UiO-66 content, but more Ni@UiO-66 with photocatalytic activity are exposed on the surface rather than in membrane bulk. In other words, M2 possesses much better light accessibility for Ni@UiO-66 than M1. Thus, the photocatalytic self-cleaning ability of M2 was greatly enhanced. The comparison of the separation membranes between prepared PES-Ni@UiO-66 membrane with experimental results in previous studies is shown in Table 2. It is clear that, the prepared PES-Ni@UiO-66 membrane in this study outperformed many previously reported membranes in both separation performance and antifouling ability. Therefore, it has certain potential in the treatment of wastewater containing natural organic matter (NOM).
## 3.5. Antifouling mechanism
The extended XDLVO theory has been proved to be feasible to analyze the forces between two interfaces, which can also be used to evaluate the interaction between the membrane and the pollutant deposition layer. The parameters used for the calculation of the XDLVO theory are shown in Table 3. It can be seen that, electron donor $(\gamma^{-})$ of M0 and M2 is $3.556\mathrm{~m~J~m~}^{-2}$ and $25.837\mathrm{~m~J~m~}$ -2, respectively. Compared with the M0, the prepared M2 owns higher hydrophilicity and more negative charge, thus it exhibits enhanced antifouling performance, which has been proved in the antifouling test. Fig. 12 shows that the total interaction energy of M2 to BSA and YEF is positive, while that of M0 to BSA and YEF is negative. Generally, the negative total interaction means that the membrane and the pollutant tend to attract each other [71]. Therefore, from a thermodynamic point of view, the original PES membrane was liable to attract BSA and YEF, resulting in much severe membrane fouling, while the adhesion of BSA and YEF on M2 is thermodynamically unfavorable. Otherwise, although the interaction energy of SA and HA is all negative, the total interaction energy of the modified M2 decreases greatly compared with M0 and M1. Thus, the M2 possesses a much better antifouling ability than M0 and M1.
## 4. Conclusions
Magnetic Ni@UiO-66 nanoparticles were synthesized through an in-situ reduction method, and then incorporated into PES membrane matrix and manipulated to membrane upper layer through an external magnetic field in phase-inversion process. The magnetic field strategy endows PES-Ni@UiO-66 membrane with more exposed hydrophilic Ni@UiO-66 nanoparticles on surface. Thus, the prepared membrane exhibits greatly improved water permeability and antifouling performance. Moreover, resulting from the photocatalytic activity ${\mathsf{N i@U i O–66}}$ , the decreased membrane flux due to severe membrane fouling can be recovered with an FRR higher than $95\%$ under UV irradiation, demonstrating fascinating photocatalytic self-cleaning ability. The antifouling mechanism analyzed by XDLVO theory illustrated that interaction energies between M2 membrane and pollutants were less attractive or even repulsive as compared with those for the other two membranes. This study provided a novel strategy regarding manipulating distribution of MOFs-based nanoparticles in membrane matrix for fabrication of high-performance membranes for water treatment. The developed PES-Ni@UiO-66 membrane reveals great application potential in the treatment of wastewater.
